CAT = "cemapFca"; SEG = "FCA"
Q = [
("The FCA's strategic objective is to ensure that the relevant markets:",
 "Function well", ["Grow every year", "Have fewer firms", "Pay lower taxes"],
 "Under FSMA the FCA's strategic objective is that relevant markets function well. It is supported by three operational objectives."),
("Which is one of the FCA's three operational objectives?",
 "Protecting consumers", ["Setting Bank Rate", "Collecting stamp duty", "Approving the Budget"],
 "The operational objectives are consumer protection, protecting the integrity of the UK financial system and promoting effective competition in consumers' interests."),
("The PRA's general objective is to promote:",
 "The safety and soundness of its firms", ["Fair treatment in mortgage advice", "Lower prices for retail consumers", "Competition between mortgage brokers"],
 "The PRA focuses on firms not failing in a way that harms the system. Fair treatment of customers is the FCA's job."),
("Which firm is regulated by both the PRA and the FCA?",
 "A building society", ["A mortgage broker", "An independent financial adviser", "A credit broker"],
 "Deposit-takers such as banks and building societies are dual-regulated: prudentially by the PRA and for conduct by the FCA. Brokers and advisers are FCA solo-regulated."),
("FSMA 2023 gave the FCA and PRA a new secondary objective relating to:",
 "UK competitiveness and growth", ["Setting the UK inflation target", "Running deposit guarantees", "Pension tax relief rules"],
 "The secondary international competitiveness and growth objective asks both regulators to support the UK economy's growth, as long as their primary objectives come first."),
("Principles-based regulation means firms are expected to:",
 "Meet broad outcomes, not only rules", ["Follow only the detailed written rules", "Write their own laws and rules", "Ignore any guidance from the FCA"],
 "High-level principles set the standard and firms decide how to meet it, so compliance with the letter of a rule is not enough if the outcome is poor."),
("Under section 19 of FSMA, carrying on a regulated activity in the UK without authorisation or exemption is:",
 "A criminal offence", ["Allowed for small firms", "A civil matter only", "Permitted for a trial period"],
 "Section 19 sets the general prohibition. Breaching it is a criminal offence, and agreements made in breach may be unenforceable against the customer."),
("A firm that the FCA authorises receives a:",
 "Part 4A permission", ["Banking licence from HM Treasury", "Certificate from the Bank of England", "Consumer credit licence from the OFT"],
 "Authorisation gives a Part 4A permission under FSMA listing the regulated activities the firm may carry on. The OFT no longer licenses credit firms."),
("An appointed representative can carry on regulated activities without its own authorisation because:",
 "Its principal takes responsibility", ["It holds an FCA exemption certificate", "It is too small to need regulating", "It deals only in cash payments"],
 "An appointed representative acts under a contract with an authorised principal, which is responsible for its regulated activities and their oversight."),
("Before authorising a firm, the FCA checks it meets the minimum standards called the:",
 "Threshold Conditions", ["Principles for Businesses", "Conduct Rules", "Statements of Principle"],
 "The Threshold Conditions in COND, such as adequate resources, suitability, effective supervision and a viable business model, must be met at authorisation and at all times after."),
("Which Handbook block contains the Principles for Businesses?",
 "High Level Standards", ["Business Standards", "Specialist Sourcebooks", "Regulatory Processes"],
 "PRIN sits in the High Level Standards block with SYSC, COND, COCON and FIT. Conduct of business sourcebooks such as COBS and MCOB are in Business Standards."),
("In the FCA Handbook, a provision marked 'G' is:",
 "Guidance", ["A binding rule", "A government decree", "A general prohibition"],
 "R marks a rule, which binds firms. G is guidance, which explains rules and is not binding in itself. E marks an evidential provision."),
("Which FCA sourcebook sets out the rules for handling client money?",
 "CASS", ["MCOB", "DISP", "COND"],
 "The Client Assets sourcebook requires client money to be held separately from the firm's own money, in designated client accounts, so it is protected if the firm fails."),
("Under the Senior Managers Regime, a senior manager must be:",
 "Approved before taking the role", ["Elected by the firm's customers", "Certified by the firm each year", "A qualified mortgage adviser"],
 "Senior management functions need regulatory approval in advance. Staff in certification functions, such as advisers, are assessed and certified by the firm itself."),
("Under the Certification Regime, who confirms that a certified employee is fit and proper?",
 "The firm", ["The FCA", "The Financial Ombudsman", "The employee's own adviser"],
 "Firms must assess and certify the fitness and propriety of staff in certification functions. The FCA does not approve them individually."),
("Which of these is an individual Conduct Rule that applies to almost all staff of a regulated firm?",
 "You must act with integrity", ["You must hold a degree", "You must report to the board", "You must be a shareholder"],
 "The individual Conduct Rules in COCON include acting with integrity, with due skill, care and diligence, openness with regulators, proper market conduct and good outcomes for retail customers."),
("Under the Training and Competence rules, a new mortgage adviser must:",
 "Be supervised until competent", ["Pass an interview with the FCA", "Have five years' experience first", "Advise only on remortgages"],
 "TC requires firms to supervise advisers until they are assessed as competent, and advisers need an appropriate qualification. Firms then keep competence under review."),
("The 'fit and proper' test for staff includes honesty, integrity and reputation, competence and capability, and:",
 "Financial soundness", ["Physical fitness", "Home ownership", "Political neutrality"],
 "The FIT sourcebook names three areas: honesty, integrity and reputation; competence and capability; and financial soundness."),
("The FCA publishes details of authorised firms and approved individuals on the:",
 "Financial Services Register", ["HM Land Registry title register", "Open electoral register", "Companies House register"],
 "The public Financial Services Register lets customers check that a firm is authorised and what it may do, which helps to avoid scams and clone firms."),
("Which sourcebook sets prudential requirements, such as professional indemnity insurance, for mortgage intermediaries?",
 "MIPRU", ["COBS", "CASS", "ICOBS"],
 "MIPRU covers prudential standards for mortgage and insurance intermediaries, including capital resources and professional indemnity insurance."),
("Which of these is an FCA enforcement power?",
 "Imposing a fine", ["Jailing an adviser", "Setting Bank Rate", "Overturning a court ruling"],
 "The FCA can fine, publicly censure, ban individuals, vary or cancel permissions and seek restitution. Prison sentences can be imposed only by a criminal court."),
("A firm that disagrees with an FCA decision notice can refer the matter to the:",
 "Upper Tribunal", ["Financial Ombudsman Service", "Supreme Court directly", "Competition and Markets Authority"],
 "Decision notices can be referred to the Upper Tribunal (Tax and Chancery Chamber), which can reconsider the case afresh."),
("Which activity is a regulated activity under the FSMA Regulated Activities Order?",
 "Advising on a regulated mortgage", ["Valuing a house for a sale", "Doing the conveyancing on a sale", "Surveying a house's structure"],
 "Advising on, arranging, entering into and administering regulated mortgage contracts are regulated activities. Conveyancing and surveying are regulated by their own professions."),
("A loan to an individual or trustee secured on UK land, where at least 40% of the land is used as a dwelling by the borrower or a relative, is a:",
 "Regulated mortgage contract", ["Commercial buy-to-let loan", "Business bridging loan", "Commercial property mortgage"],
 "This is the core definition of a regulated mortgage contract, the main type of lending covered by MCOB. Since March 2016 it covers second charge loans as well as first charges. Most buy-to-let loans fall outside it."),
("Prudential rules mainly aim to make sure a firm has enough:",
 "Capital and liquidity", ["Staff in its branches", "Budget for advertising", "Products to choose from"],
 "Prudential standards require firms to hold enough capital to absorb losses and enough liquid assets to meet obligations, so they do not fail in a disorderly way."),
]
